  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "src-07-root-equations-missing-values",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Root Equations and Missing Values",
    "description": "Isolate a root or missing radicand, undo the square root by squaring, and check the value in the original equation.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the equation habit from a direct root through roots of quotients, roots containing x², missing radicands, decimal products, two-root equations, scaled root ratios and a BODMAS capstone. The repeated routine is isolate → square → solve → substitute back.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach the equation patterns used throughout the chapter: √x=k; √(A/x)=k; √(A+x²)=d; a missing radicand inside ordinary arithmetic; and decimal products under a root. Build the habit of isolating the radical before squaring and substituting the answer back. Include the solved-example pattern √86.49+√(5+x²)=12.3, a scale relation where a root of a quotient is known, and a capstone that combines a root equation with ordinary BODMAS. Keep equations at the elementary level used in the chapter and use the positive square root unless the source question explicitly asks otherwise.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 30"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the safest way to solve an equation that contains a square root.\n\nDo not square too early. First get the root by itself, then square, solve the ordinary equation, and put the answer back to check it.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A root equation can be undone by squaring, but only after the root is isolated.\n\n√x = 13\n\nSquare both sides:\nx = 169",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check in the original equation:\n√169 = 13 ✓\n\nThe check matters because squaring is an operation that can create extra algebraic possibilities in harder equations.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
